WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,466
A practical system hardening routine starts with inventory, ownership, and classification.

2
00:00:06,466 --> 00:00:16,327
Know the workstations, laptops, servers, cloud instances, containers, appliances, administrative systems, and unmanaged devices in scope.

3
00:00:16,327 --> 00:00:23,360
Identify the owner, business purpose, criticality, data sensitivity, exposure, and support status.

4
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:26,108
That context drives the rest of the work.

5
00:00:26,108 --> 00:00:30,068
Apply tested baselines and reduce unnecessary exposure.

6
00:00:30,068 --> 00:00:34,271
Use realistic configuration standards for each system role.

7
00:00:34,271 --> 00:00:43,243
Remove unused software, disable unnecessary services, restrict remote access, review local accounts, and document exceptions.

8
00:00:43,243 --> 00:00:47,770
Keep required functions reliable while reducing avoidable risk.

9
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:54,155
Hardening should make systems easier to understand and defend, not harder to operate safely.

10
00:00:54,155 --> 00:00:57,065
Control privilege and maintain software.

11
00:00:57,065 --> 00:01:11,049
Use standard user access by default, limit administrative rights, review privileged groups, manage service accounts, protect emergency access, patch operating systems and third-party applications,

12
00:01:11,049 --> 00:01:16,868
replace unsupported software, and keep endpoint protection enabled and monitored.

13
00:01:16,868 --> 00:01:22,365
Connect patching decisions to inventory, classification, and change control.

14
00:01:22,365 --> 00:01:25,921
Finally, monitor, recover, validate, and improve.

15
00:01:25,921 --> 00:01:38,854
Collect useful logs, review endpoint alerts, track backup status, test recovery, validate baseline application, manage change windows, preserve rollback plans, and review exceptions.

16
00:01:38,854 --> 00:01:51,948
System hardening is practical risk reduction through disciplined operations: inventory, classify, baseline, reduce exposure, control privilege, patch, monitor, validate, and improve.
